Chapter 10, Section 32: "――Aaaaaaah"



"So, it's not like she actually created an entire alternate space with Yin Magic, right?"


"If she could do that, Mother would be able to create worlds as she pleases. No matter how amazing Mother is, she wouldn't have enough mana for that."


"Ah, so it's a mana issue? If that could be cleared, she could do things like a creator god?"


"It wouldn't be strange if she could... That's how incredible Mother is."


Walking through the ruined castle, Beatrice spoke proudly of her astonishing mother's power.

Perhaps because Subaru had assumed it was an old wound and hadn't delved too deeply into stories of the past until now, Beatrice's boasting about her beloved mother continued as if she had endless things to say.

It probably also helped that this was her first return home in four hundred years, actually walking through Kugelschreiber Castle. ——Even if it was a sight where the appearance of days past had been mercilessly destroyed.


"Still, seeing you feel that way about Beako makes me a little jealous."


"Honestly, Subaru and Mother compete in entirely different categories. Mother is the unshakable number one in the family rankings, and Subaru is the unshakable number one in the Betty rankings."


"Wait, what's that? That makes me super happy. Does that mean I beat Puck?"


"He's tied for first place with me."


"Damn that fluffy bastard...! He stands in my way both for being Emilia-tan's number one AND Beako's number one...!"


"Nyafufu." The image of the cat spirit stroking his whiskers with a smug face floated into Subaru's mind, and he gritted his teeth at the height of the enemy he must overcome. He had to be careful, since he himself was also charmed by that fur.

And, during that exchange——,


"——It's here."


Beatrice stopped abruptly, and "Here?" Subaru and the others tilted their heads.

After all, what she had turned to face was an unremarkable wall in a corridor. Within the castle, it was just one wall in a passage they had advanced through while watching their footing on the crumbling floor.

However, Beatrice said "Tsk tsk tsk," wagging a raised finger left and right.


"The owner of this castle is a 'Witch.' To think that the records left by that 'Witch' would just be sitting in an ordinary room is utterly absurd. Therefore——"


Saying that, Beatrice gently touched the wall with the finger she had been wagging.

Nothing happened. With her finger still against the wall, Beatrice reached out her other hand and called softly, "Subaru, Subaru." He hurriedly grasped that hand.

Mana flowed into Beatrice through Subaru, and light emerged from the tip of the finger touching the wall. It formed a door of light upon the empty wall——,


"This is the same kind we saw once at the castle entrance."


"If the best thing already exists, there's no reason to deliberately make a different one. Now, take a good look. ——This is the private research laboratory of the 'Witch' Echidna!"


Beyond the wall that had become passable, what greeted Subaru and the others as they finally stepped inside was air that felt heavier than when they first entered the castle, the scent of timber that had accumulated long silence, and a room whose contents were far too befitting of a 'Witch.'


"This is... quite something."


"Kuhahaha, the Duchess's choice of words is so graceful and reserved! Something like this is creepy! In bad taste! Gloomy! Saying it outright would be for her own good! Oops, but she's already dead, isn't she?"


"K-Kuroinu! What a way to speak! Librarian, I'm so sorry!"


At the rant of 'Kuroinu,' who had spun around while surveying the interior, 'Hakuyou' hurriedly bowed her head in apology. But Beatrice simply raised her palm magnanimously and said, "It's fine."


"Being feared and held in awe is also necessary for a 'Witch'... Rather, this reaction is exactly what Mother would have expected. There's no need to apologize."


"Man, your feelings toward your parent are seriously twisted..."


"Eh, 'Long-Tongue'-kun is the one saying that...?"


Ferris voiced a simple question at Rachins, who had twisted his lips.

Indeed, Rachins had deliberately left a wealthy home to hang around with delinquent friends. Even if not about parents specifically, there was strong suspicion of family troubles.

In any case——,


"'Kuroinu's' way of putting it is somewhat offensive, but it is certainly something new."


Yes, even Russell affirmed 'Kuroinu's' expression as such a scene spread before them.

The laboratory was a large space, with a high ceiling supported by numerous black pillars, that could rightly be called a great hall. Several pedestals resembling workbenches lined the room, but what caught the eye were the countless crystals of all sizes and colors upon them——,


"...This really feels like a 'Witch's' laboratory."


Peering closely at a nearby crystal, Subaru murmured quietly.

Before his eyes, within a palm-sized crystal, a butterfly with its wings spread was sealed. Beside it were plants preserved not just with petals but even the roots extending into the soil, and next to that, a small lizard-like creature curled up.

All were specimens preserved without losing color or shape, looking as if they might start moving at any moment.


"It's not just small ones; there are demon beasts here too. Take a look. That one, for example, is a large... what was it called?"


"This is surprising. A Guiltylowe. To think demon beasts could be preserved in this form."


Among those viewing the crystal specimens, Crusch and Russell, who had gone deeper in, expressed admiration. Before the two of them was a crystal the size of a dragon carriage, and within it was the form of a large demon beast.

Beyond that, there were fish, birds, demon beasts they had never seen before—the variety of specimens was extensive.


"All these living creatures, for what purpose... Hyaah!"


"Oi oi, you idiot! Don't touch things without permission! That's recklessness and carelessness at its finest!"


"S-Sorry! But, um, look at this!"


Letting out a small shriek, 'Hakuyou,' having been scolded by 'Kuroinu,' pointed at a crystal she had apparently poked lightly. Inside was a crimson-furred red deer, but it wasn't merely sealed within. ——Light arose within the crystal, and the inside of the red deer's body became transparent, revealing its organs, bones, and even countless lines of light running through it.


"Wait, is that showing not just the inside of the body, but the gate too?"


Witnessing that, Ferris murmured in a voice that did not hide his shudder of horror. His yellow eyes wide, he touched a crystal near himself and confirmed the same phenomenon.


"How is it, Ferris? What can you tell?"


"...The children inside aren't alive. But it's amazing. When something dies, its od slowly disperses, but this has the shape of the od from when it was alive copied exactly."


"Copying the od... meaning, the shape of the soul is recorded?"


"Yes. ...At least, I believe it's something close to that."


Perhaps the shock was too great, Ferris seemed not even to notice that he was being addressed by Crusch, trembling at this glimpse of the 'Witch's' research.

Subaru also looked toward Beatrice at that explanation.


"It seems the signboard of 'Witch's laboratory' is no lie. Beako, haven't you heard what she was doing here?"


"...Mother didn't like showing her research to others. Even the knowledge she gave to others was carefully selected. Probably, she had unpleasant memories related to it."


"Unpleasant memories... Well, if you're smart or strong, all sorts of things happen."


Thinking about it, there was a gap in thinking between the young Dona who had boasted about being sought for advice by others, and the parent Dona that Beatrice spoke of. It suggested that the young Dona's unpleasant experiences and resultant change in values had produced the parent Dona over time—,


"These experimental tools are interesting, but ideally we'd find documents we can decipher here. 'Librarian,' do you have any idea where?"


"There's a small room further in that I was told not to enter."


"A small room—this one?"


Prompted by Russell that staring at experimental equipment wouldn't get them anywhere, Beatrice pointed to a corner of the laboratory. Subaru put his hand on the door there and opened it. Immediately, a heavy scent of old books wafted out, and he reflexively waved a hand in front of his face.

And then——,


"Oi oi, this is... where are we even supposed to start? This is rough."


From behind the murmuring Subaru, the others who peered inside likewise groaned.

The room Beatrice had described as small was certainly small compared to the great-hall-sized laboratory, but it was still about the size of the dining hall at Roswaal Mansion.

Within a room of that size, books, bundles of paper, and scrolls were crammed in haphazardly, with barely any space to stand.


"I did say it. ——Mother wasn't very good at tidying up."


"I wish there'd been a limit to it. Think of future generations."


Picking up two books that had fallen right near the door at his feet, Subaru felt despair upon finding that both were untitled and apparently unrelated and discontinuous with each other.


"First the 'Forbidden Library,' then the Pleiades Watchtower..."


Natsuki Subaru had no good memories of other people's archives, and it seemed this one would also be filed into the same category of memories—a truly troublesome library.



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



——In the end, organizing the 'Witch's' library took several hours.


The work undertaken by all eight of them continued with a series of fine but major tasks: first righting fallen bookshelves, gathering fallen books, collecting scattered bundles of paper, carefully retrieving torn scrolls.

Only after all that were they finally at the starting line of organizing. Truly, one should never volunteer to tidy up someone else's library. The silver lining was that, at minimum, there was no chance of Subaru and the others overlooking anything they needed.


"——This one, and this one. Also, put these documents in a box. We'll take them back and examine them."


So saying, Russell stared intently at the recovered items that had been sorted as much as possible, selecting the valuable ones—using his 'Appraisal Divine Protection' to choose which materials to take.

The selected materials had no common format, and the characters, diagrams, and such recorded in them were completely inconsistent.


"That last book seems like nothing but an endless flower observation diary... does it have any value?"


"I don't know. But if I don't begin by believing it does, I cannot live with these eyes. In fact, there was meaning in not giving up on you all, wasn't there?"


"Hah, well said. Even though you were about to throw us away until I spoke up."


"Yes, and the reason I listened to your words was precisely because you had value."


"This guy seriously pisses me off...!"


Having been the one to start with sarcasm, Rachins ended up on the receiving end, grinding his teeth.

Despite being made to go along with Russell's important matters, the more time passed, the more friction seemed to grow between the two rather than any familiarity.

However——,


"Ah—, ugh! Done! Cleaned up! What a tiring, arduous day! I've decided! From now on, anyone who looks like they'd read a lot of books—I'll kill them before they buy a lot of books!"


"It's not necessarily the case that one has opportunities to examine the libraries of those who hold many books."


"It's no use saying anything, 'Suirei'-sama. That's just 'Kuroinu'-kun's usual loose talk."


"Is that so. Then I said something unnecessary. Continue as you like."


"Like I'd do it! I'm exhausted, worn out!"


Throwing himself onto a workbench, 'Kuroinu' wailed like a large child. His mannerisms were childish but his large frame made it anything but cute; still, Subaru sympathized with his complaint of fatigue.


"The time limit of one night is still in effect, and just because this is an alternate space, there's no benefit like time flowing slower than outside, right?"


"As far as Betty knows, no. If there were such a benefit, this castle might have remained in a slightly better state... though with 'Melancholy' involved, even that is doubtful."


The neglect and aging likely played a part, but most of the castle's ruined state was due to the rampage of the 'Mage of Melancholy' here. There had probably been a clash between 'Witch' and 'Mage' here that couldn't be measured by simply calling it a parent-child quarrel.


"Let's take the results back and confirm them. 'Librarian,' are there materials outside this room?"


"There shouldn't be. For the same reason she didn't keep that sort of book in the 'Forbidden Library,' she wouldn't have placed them anywhere but this room."


As she answered Russell, Beatrice glanced at Subaru, and he nodded back.

If the theory that Echidna had not wanted to show Beatrice books related to 'souls' was adopted, then the library that Beatrice had been forbidden to enter was exactly the hiding place for those forbidden books.

If that was certain, then all that remained was to take the harvest back——,


"——Hey, 'Librarian'-chan, is that all?"


"Ferris?"


At Ferris's sudden question with his head tilted, Subaru frowned.

He understood Ferris's desperate feelings, but Beatrice's cooperative attitude had been clear throughout. Having that doubted was something Subaru couldn't overlook.

But before Subaru could say anything, Ferris bent down to meet Beatrice's eyes.


"This is a place you haven't been to in a long time, right? And even just now, while 'Gingitsune' was comparing the materials, you kept glancing outside the room."


"Eh. W-Was that true, Beako?"


"Mm... i-it's nothing. Subaru and Ferris are both overthinking things."


Beatrice waved her hands in a fluster at Subaru and Ferris peering into her face. It was an adorable resistance, but——,


"——A wind of lies is blowing."


Crusch, narrowing her uncovered eye, easily exposed the lie.

At that point, Beatrice tried to endure with "Gununu," but eventually sighed as if giving up.


"It's nothing. I just remembered. When I was driven out of the castle by 'Melancholy,' there was no time to pack leisurely. Something I couldn't take with me might still be in my room in this castle... is all."


"I see. Is it something important?"


"It wouldn't be a problem if I didn't have it. ...But it was a present from Mother."


With downcast eyes, Beatrice concluded endearingly.

Struck by Beatrice's demeanor, Subaru also tasted frustration—at his own inadequacy in not noticing Beatrice's lingering regret before Ferris did.

But moping about it was foolish.


"Russell-san, can we go retrieve a forgotten item from four hundred years ago? I fully understand it's unrelated to the operation..."


"If you understand, then I'd like you to exercise restraint. Have you forgotten our mission?"


"I've got no comeback for that..."


"But the reason we've gotten this far is thanks to 'Librarian'-chan, right? So it's not great if 'Librarian'-chan doesn't get even one reward, is it?"


It was Ferris who once again came to Subaru's aid when he was pushed back by sound logic. At Ferris voicing an opinion favoring Beatrice, Russell directed a slightly sharp gaze at him.


"——. Surely, you don't intend this as retaliation, 'Seija'?"


"That's really not it. But don't forget, okay? 'Haisei'-kun and 'Librarian'-chan aren't your subordinates. Even so, even though they have someone they want to meet, the two of them have gone along with you for two months. Isn't it about time you showed a little something? Good faith."


"You..."

With Ferris's characteristically sharp tongue, now aimed not at himself but at someone else, Subaru felt both admiration and bewilderment at how reliable it felt.

He didn't understand why Ferris was being so considerate toward them. Of course, no matter how bad his mouth was, Subaru trusted the righteousness of the heart of one who had mastered healing magic.


"Don't give me that look. It's just, if there's a forgotten item that can be recovered, it's better to recover it, right?"


"That's... yeah. Sorry. You really are a good guy."


"——. I hate that side of you, Subaru-kyun."


Ferris turned his face away with a huff, and Subaru scratched his cheek with a wry smile. Meanwhile, Russell touched his chin beard with a troubled expression for a while at Ferris's request.


"Um, 'Gingitsune,' I'd like to ask as well. We don't know when we'll be able to come here next. It would be too sad for 'Librarian'-san."


"'Hakuyou,' you too?"


"Our circumstances should be different from 'Haisei'-san and the others. Even you cannot force them. You already understand that, don't you?"


With her cheeks tensed, even 'Hakuyou' provided supporting fire for Subaru and the others.

Though she was a deeply compassionate person, speaking up to her superior Russell must have taken considerable courage. Whether or not Subaru found that attitude admirable—,


"——Do not linger. Retrieve the item, and rejoin us promptly."


After a brief closing of his eyes, they succeeded in drawing a concession from Russell.



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



"I will prioritize packing the materials. Gather at the entrance."


"Just so you know, I'm not going either. Like I'd babysit a kid's trip down memory lane."


And so, leaving behind Russell, who prioritized work, and Rachins, who was uncooperative, the remaining members set off through Kugelschreiber Castle toward Beatrice's private room.

Unexpectedly, many had said they would accompany Subaru and the others, leaving Rachins—who ended up alone with Russell—watching them go with a look of miscalculation.


"Still, who'd have thought you'd come along too."


"Kuhahaha, it's just that this beats helping carry luggage—that's all there is to it. Besides, if that clumsy, foolish 'Hakuyou' tripped and accidentally fell through a hole, that'd be terrible, wouldn't it?"


"Y-You don't need to worry about that! I'm not that clumsy... kyah!"


"See, laugh, scold! She does it right after saying it!"


Laughing loudly, 'Kuroinu' grabbed the collar of 'Hakuyou,' who had nearly fallen, and set her upright.

The way 'Hakuyou' fulfilled the expected trope so perfectly made Subaru smile wryly, but despite his words, 'Kuroinu' was quite protective of her.

Usually, 'Hakuyou' seemed more like the caretaker, but now the roles appeared reversed.


"You two have been together since before joining 'Six Tongues'... and were 'Beloved Children,' right?"


"...I was. 'Kuroinu' too, probably."


"Probably? That part's vague?"


At 'Hakuyou's' uncertain answer, Subaru raised an eyebrow in puzzlement. He had assumed that 'Hakuyou' and 'Kuroinu' were in 'Six Tongues' under the same circumstances.


"Oho, lie or truth, truth or lie! This woman with a hypocrite's face—the truth is she's a wicked liar who just loooves deceiving you all!"


"A wind of lies is blowing. Your words are bad; 'Hakuyou's' words are good."


"Kah—! Boring, dull, and disappointing!"


'Kuroinu's' inserted jest was cut down by Crusch's Divine Protection. But that also seemed like a follow-up to divert attention from 'Hakuyou's' statement.

Indeed, Crusch had guaranteed that 'Hakuyou's' words were truth.


"Actually, I don't really understand my relationship with 'Kuroinu' either. Someone close to me... that much should be certain, but I can't grasp it concretely."


"For the record, I don't understand at all either! Me, it's like—before I knew it, I was there. And this one, it's like—she just happened to be there."


"What's that? That makes no sense at all."


"Yes, it makes no sense at all. ——I think that's my reason."


Clenching her white fist, 'Hakuyou' spoke with a somewhat resolute expression.

Having brought up the topic himself, Subaru felt slightly hesitant about whether he should have heard this. Once again, he had rudely stepped into someone's heart with his shoes on.


"No matter how much 'treading into people's hearts with shoes on' is a family precept of ours, even the one doing the treading should have manners about it, so when it happens without meaning to, it's panic-inducing."


"What are you mumbling about? Come along now, don't get lost."


His pensive arm was pulled by Beatrice, and he followed as she led the group.

Initially, Beatrice had been somewhat apologetic about having everyone accompany her on a search for her forgotten item, but as she walked through the familiar halls of her family home, her stiffness gradually eased.

Now, her eyes shone with anticipation for the reunion with the forgotten item she would soon reclaim.


"By the way, what was the present from Echidna... from your mother?"


"Hmm, that's something to look forward to when you see it. This habit of wanting to know everything impatiently isn't unique to Subaru—it's a bad habit of all humans."


"Well, anyone compared to Beako, who has a four-hundred-year track record of waiting, would end up like that."


Subaru smiled wryly at Beatrice, who puffed her chest proudly and led the way with small steps. Watching her swaying drill twin-tails and her bouncing gait, there was nothing but warmth.

The others must have felt the same; no one tried to dampen Beatrice's anticipation as she looked forward to retrieving a present four hundred years overdue.


"Even so, she just had to go and wreck the place..."


What Subaru grumbled about was a complaint against the 'Mage of Melancholy,' whose destruction had left deep scars no matter where they headed in the castle.

Every time they found where 'Melancholy's' rampage had collapsed ceilings, broken pillars, and split floors, they had to detour around impassable paths, making their way to Beatrice's room by roundabout routes.

Just now again, they crossed through a half-collapsed room and tried to shortcut through a crushed corridor—where they encountered a thick pillar lying sideways, blocking the passage.


"Oi oi, another detour?"


"——. No, look. It seems we can slip past beside the pillar."


The pillar seemed too thick for an adult to wrap both arms around, but as Beatrice pointed out, there was a gap between it and the right wall, just wide enough for one person at a time to pass through.


"That said, not being able to see the other side is worrying. I'll go first. Beako, wait your turn."


"Betty is more compact and cute, so she could pass through without difficulty."


"My flexibility isn't bad either, you know. I do pigeon pose every night."


With yoga spirit asserted, Beatrice yielded the first penguin position to Subaru.

Lowering his head, Subaru squeezed his body into the gap beside the pillar and slipped through to the other side. Fortunately, the snagging and pitfall traps he had feared did not occur; the corridor seemed to continue ahead and wasn't a dead end.


"Okay. We can proceed. Be careful not to snag your hair or dress."


Brushing off the knees of his jersey where they had touched the floor, Subaru called out to Beatrice on the other side of the pillar. He crouched down, waiting for Beatrice to come through the gap——,


"——Beako?"


Beatrice had a small body. She should have been able to slip through the gap with less difficulty than Subaru, yet no matter how long he waited, her figure did not come through. Growing impatient, he peered into the gap beside the pillar—but Beatrice was not there. No, that wasn't it.


"——H-hey, no way?"


Doubting his own eyes, Subaru lowered his head again and this time squeezed back through the gap in the opposite direction. Frustratingly scraping his head against the pillar, he emerged into the corridor on the other side and raised his face.

And then——,


"——No one... is here?"


Standing in the corridor where, just moments ago, he had been walking with everyone, Subaru looked around in stunned disbelief. Beatrice, who should have been there—Crusch, Ferris, 'Hakuyou,' 'Kuroinu'—none of them were there.

Not a single member who had come with him was there. There was no trace of them whatsoever.


"That's... that's absurd... Beatrice! Beako! Answer me! Beako—!!"


Raising his voice, Subaru called Beatrice's name loudly. His voice echoed hollowly through the ancient castle, but no reply came.

The surging anxiety instantly drenched Subaru's back in cold sweat.


"Crusch-san! Ferris!! 'Hakuyou'-chan! 'Kuroinu'! Where are you! Beako! Anyone! Anyone at all! Answer me!!"


Unable to contain himself, Subaru called out the names of his companions one after another. But there was no reply from anyone. Not just Beatrice—no reply from Crusch and the other four either. ——That was an abnormality far too obvious to ignore.


"What on earth... Could it be, Echidna!?"


This was the castle of the 'Witch of Greed,' and upon discovering the hidden materials room—not a library—he had harbored a bad feeling that some kind of mechanism might be in place.

However, since Beatrice hadn't mentioned anything about it, and since both the 'Trials' and the 'Exams' before had always had some trigger for events to occur,

his vigilance had gradually thinned——,


"Idiot, me. No, I am an idiot... letting my guard down against Echidna!"


Biting his lip hard, Subaru chastised himself with pain. There was no time to waste on lengthy self-reproach. He needed to search for the vanished Beatrice and the others immediately. Or perhaps regroup with Russell and the others.

And he had to overcome the mechanisms of this Kugelschreiber Castle——,


"——Huh?"


It happened right after he resolved to do so.

As he took his first step to run back the way he had come, it occurred. ——Without any sign, the footing he stepped on gave way without resistance, and his body tilted.

By the time he thought "Bad," it was too late. ——The next instant, his body was hurled into the air.


"——!"


The floor gave way, and he was flung outside the corridor. The moment he realized that, Subaru pulled the Guiltywhip from behind his waist and desperately lashed it at the corridor he had slipped through. ——Fortunately, the tip caught on a crack or something that had formed in the passage.

"Guoh!" A violent impact struck his right shoulder holding the whip, and Subaru's body caught.


"Wh-wh-wha..."


He had no idea what had happened.

Without sign, without omen, without warning, without any trigger, the floor had collapsed. Thrown outside the corridor, Subaru looked around—and shuddered.


——Nothing. It was an empty void, as if the creation of heaven and earth had never happened there.


"————"


Beatrice had said at the very beginning that if you fell, you could never return. The meaning of that was now crystal clear to Subaru, hanging halfway into the fall.

Indeed, it was impossible. If he fell here, he couldn't return. There was no way to return. He wouldn't be found.

Therefore——,


"Somehow, I have to climb back up..."


It was an unreliable lifeline, merely caught in a crack in the floor.

So that it wouldn't come loose, he wrapped the whip around his wrist and, little by little but still urgently, carefully reeled his own body up, up, up——,


"Eh."


The snapping impact was sudden.

If rendered into sound, it would be a snap. But he heard no sound. There was no room for that.


"——A."


Mercilessly, the lifeline supporting Subaru's body lost its tension, and as if mocking the desperately struggling life, it cast Subaru's body into the void.


"A-aaaaaaaaah——!!"


Screaming, Subaru's body spun and spun and spun vertically, backward, losing all sense of up and down, as he rapidly receded from the corridor—no, from Kugelschreiber Castle.

Receding, receding, receding, falling. Into a literally pitch-dark abyss.


"I... sa..." *


Battered by fierce wind and pummeled by tremendous force, a voice spilled out.

He saw it. He had seen it with his own eyes. At the moment the whip came loose and he was thrown out, he had seen it. ——With a snap, the tip of the whip that had come free from the corridor, exposing a fresh, sharp cut surface.


Someone had cut Subaru's whip, severing his lifeline.

Severed, falling, falling, falling, falling, falling falling falling falling falling falling fallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfallingfalling——,


"————"


It wouldn't come.


Clenching his teeth, Subaru tensed his entire body for the coming impact with the ground.

He tried to search for a way out, and immediately realized there was none to be had. Beatrice wasn't there. There was nowhere to reach with his whip. He had already been separated from the castle by dozens, hundreds of meters.

There was no way back, no means of rescue. So all that remained was to prepare for the 'death' that should come, and above all, to steel himself desperately to share this crisis with everyone.


Come. Come anytime. Come. Come right now. Come now. Come. Quickly, right away—everyone is in danger. Come right now. Come. Come. Come. Come on. Come!


"————"


——It wouldn't come.


"————"


He realized it. He realized it after steeling himself.

He should have understood it himself earlier. This was a world where the creation of heaven and earth had never occurred. No matter how far he fell, there was no earth anywhere that would collide with him and shatter Subaru's body to pieces.

Even if he kept falling like this, even if he fell forever and ever, he would never arrive anywhere.


"————"


He desperately tried to deny the terrifying possibility that surfaced in his mind.

But the greatest ally of denial—the collision with the ground—did not come. Still, the wind and the fall continued to toss Subaru's body about, and its speed only increased.


Eventually, his vision blacked out.

The rotation of his body disrupted his blood circulation, and blood rushed excessively to his head and the extremities of his limbs. Intense pain began to race through his entire body, and with that, Subaru's consciousness——,



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



"————"


It wouldn't end. This fall had no end.


Subaru didn't know how much time he had spent accepting that fact.

His falling body simply would not stop rotating, and the resulting disruption of blood flow caused him to lose consciousness countless times, then regain it without any trigger, each time forced to recognize the nightmare situation as reality.


His stomach cried out in hunger, his lower body—having lost bladder control—had no sensation, and his eyes had long since become useless.


Even so, Natsuki Subaru was alive. He was, unfortunately, alive.


"——Aaaaaaah."


Groaning lowly, Subaru cursed his own slowness of judgment, his own foolishness.

His body, endlessly falling and battered by the wind, had lost its freedom; his limbs no longer moved as he willed. While his hands could still move, he might have been able to strangle himself.

But he could no longer do that. No such strength remained anywhere. Likewise, not even the strength to bite his tongue remained—every means of leading himself to death had been lost.


"——Aaaaaaah."


Even now, while Subaru was pointlessly falling, Beatrice and the others were in danger.

Within a castle he could never return to, Beatrice and the others might still be exposed to threat. ——And yet, he couldn't return to them. He couldn't even die.


"——Aaaaaaah."


No matter how much Subaru wished for an end, his lungs demanded air, his heart pumped blood through a body with disrupted circulation, and the minimal functions of life continued.

Time in which he could do nothing, could not even die, was stretched out endlessly, pointlessly, meaninglessly.


"——Aaaaaaah."


He was thirsty.

Having screamed, shed tears, and leaked urine, having drained all the moisture from his body, he was parched.


His body was freezing.

His blood flow was disordered, he had spent an eternity of time being battered by wind without a single motion to generate heat.


His soul was screaming.

For Beatrice, for everyone at the castle, for Emilia, for his scattered companions—it cried out for them.


"——Aaaaaaah."


A voice—not a scream, not a wail, but a groaning voice—continued to escape him.

From the depths of his body, from the deepest, deepest, deepest bottom, as if wringing it out, he continued to produce a voice.


Quickly, even one second sooner, to wring it all out, so that he could die.


There was nothing else he could do. ——No, there was. There was something besides groaning. It was to decide. To make a firm vow in his heart, to decide, and to carry it out without fail.


He would not let go. He would absolutely not let go. He would not let go of Beatrice.


"——Aaaaaaah."


No matter what, he would absolutely not let go.


"——Aaaaaaah."


So, making that firm vow in his heart, Natsuki Subaru continued to wring himself out.

Drop by drop, until the very last drop, as if squeezing it all out——.

